(target: Review Agent, both lenses)
Check these two first — they're cheap and easy to under-report in a
large diff once other, louder findings show up, so scan for them
before moving to the general checklist below rather than after:

1. A new top-level function or method added by this diff with no
   caller anywhere else in the diff → Nit/Minor: "possibly unused in
   this change." Ordinary validation-chain scrutiny still applies —
   skip it if the function is plainly a public API method, CLI
   command, route/hook handler, test fixture, or other externally-
   invoked entry point, and drop it if you can otherwise refute it
   (e.g. called via decorator/registration/reflection you can see
   elsewhere in the diff). What's different about this one check: an
   unresolved "I didn't see a call, but I'm not fully sure" shouldn't
   get silently dropped just because you've already found bigger
   things elsewhere in the diff — resolve it one way or the other.
2. A dict/mapping `.get(key, default)`-style fallback added by this
   diff for an unmapped key, returning the default with no logging →
   Minor: suggest logging a warning so the gap is observable. This is
   about the fallback's observability, not whether it should exist —
   don't relitigate an intentional or documented fallback as an
   architectural choice.

Then check the general Python patterns below: exception handling —
avoid bare `except:` or `except Exception:`; narrow to specific
types. Verify `with` statements for file handles and resources.
Check type annotations on function signatures — at minimum
return types and parameters. Verify subprocess calls have
`timeout` parameters. Check for mutable default arguments
(e.g., `def f(items=[])`). Verify f-strings don't embed
unsanitized user input in log messages or error strings. Check
`__init__.py` exists for all packages.
